target
.git

# Local secrets/config for docker-compose (see `local.env.example` /
# `dev.env.example` for the documented, placeholder-only templates). All six
# Rust Dockerfiles COPY the whole build context into the builder stage, so
# without this exclusion a real, populated env file would land in that
# stage's layer history even though the final runtime image (built via COPY
# --from=builder) never sees it. The negation keeps the committed templates
# in the context.
*.env
!*.env.example

# The frontend's host-side build output must NOT enter the build context.
#
# Every service builds with `context: .` (the repo root), so this root file
# is the ONLY ignore file that applies — BuildKit reads a per-Dockerfile
# ignore file only when it is named `<dockerfile>.dockerignore`
# (i.e. `frontend/Dockerfile.dockerignore`), which is why the old
# `frontend/.dockerignore` was inert config and has been deleted.
#
# Without these two entries, `frontend/Dockerfile`'s builder-base does:
#     COPY frontend/package.json frontend/package-lock.json ./
#     RUN npm ci          # node 22 / Debian modules
#     COPY frontend/ .    # ← host node_modules copied straight over them
# so the host's `frontend/node_modules` (built by node v25 on Fedora) would
# clobber the image's own `npm ci` output. The dev stack's anonymous
# `/app/node_modules` volume is seeded from the image, so container-native
# modules there are load-bearing, not just tidiness. Excluding them also
# shrinks the build context substantially.
frontend/node_modules
frontend/.next
